ProcessedDate

Example:

ProcessingStatus = Completed
GeneratedExcel = User Account Reactivation-125-20260909-074512.xlsx
GeneratedPDF = User Account Reactivation-125-20260909-074512.pdf
ProcessedDate = 09/09/2026 07:45

If something fails:

ProcessingStatus = Error
ProcessingError = Signature file not found: Bong Gakaday.png

This makes support much easier.


---

42. Important Issue With Multiple Records Per Reference No.

There's one important business consideration.

You said multiple SharePoint records can have:

Reference No = GNS-002

For example:

GNS-002 | 001 | Juan
GNS-002 | 002 | Bong
GNS-002 | 003 | Pedro

If your PowerApps creates three SharePoint items separately, the trigger:

When an item is created

will potentially fire three times.

That could produce:

PDF 1
PDF 2
PDF 3

for the same:

GNS-002

even though each flow run retrieves the same three users.

Best production solution

Ideally, have the Reactivation process create a single "request header" record, or add a field such as:

GenerationStatus

and ensure only the designated first/request record generates the document.

If your PowerApps creates all records as one batch, this is especially important to address.


---

43. Concurrency

There are actually two separate concurrency issues:

Excel overwrite

Solved by unique filenames:

Request ID + Timestamp

Duplicate processing

Solved by:

ProcessingStatus

or a request/header record.

Power Automate also provides trigger concurrency control. By default, trigger concurrency is off; Microsoft documents enabling it when you need to control simultaneous runs. 

For your design, I would not rely on concurrency control as the main protection. Unique filenames are better because different requests should be allowed to run at the same time.


---

44. Another Important Excel Best Practice

Your Office Script should use:

workbook.getWorksheet("Reactivation Form 2")

instead of:

workbook.getActiveWorksheet()

because Power Automate runs Office Scripts in an independent Excel session and there may be no meaningful "active" worksheet. Microsoft specifically recommends avoiding relative references such as getActiveWorksheet() in Power Automate scripts. 

Your script above already follows this best practice.


---

45. Date Handling

Power Automate uses UTC, while Excel doesn't have a fixed inherent timezone. If the dates in your SharePoint list are date/time columns, make sure you format them deliberately before sending them to the script. Microsoft documents this Power Automate/Excel timezone difference. 

For example:

formatDateTime(
    triggerBody()?['RequestDate'],
    'MM/dd/yyyy'
)

and:

formatDateTime(
    triggerBody()?['ReactivationDate'],
    'MM/dd/yyyy'
)

If you only need the date, this is preferable to passing the raw ISO timestamp.


